  "name": "Lagrangian measurements in the Kamchatka Current and Oyashio",
  "description": "From 1988 to 1993, 23 satellite-tracked drifting buoys entered the Kamchatka Current. The buoy trajectories showed a well-formed, high-speed current that originated near Shirshov Ridge, and flowed southward through Kamchatka Strait. During some years, the buoys turned eastward at ~50°N, while in other years they were transported as far south as Japan (40°N). Only one buoy entered the Sea of Okhotsk. Eddies were evident in many of the buoy trajectories. Greatest maximum daily velocities (> 100 cm s) were observed south of Kamchatka Strait, with 50-60 cm s being more common.\n\nDeposited content: descriptive metadata only; underlying research data are not included.\n\nPurpose: To form a Lagrangian profile of satellite-tracked drifting buoys entered the Kamchatka Current from 1998 to 1993.",
  "url": "https://zenodo.org/records/23508590",
